%[text] # Ultrasound Imaging
%[text] In the ultrasound lecture, we saw how a transducer sends out pulses of sound and listens for the echoes. In this script, we put numbers to those ideas: we calculate depths from echo times, work out how much sound reflects at each tissue boundary, see how attenuation limits depth, and calculate a Doppler shift. Then we look at real ultrasound images: 3D ultrasound volumes of a phantom and a forearm, and a color Doppler echocardiogram.
%[text] ### Learning Objectives
%[text] After this exercise, you should be able to
%[text] 1. Calculate the depth of a reflector from its echo time
%[text] 2. Explain how differences in acoustic impedance create echoes, and why we use gel
%[text] 3. Explain how attenuation and transducer frequency trade depth against resolution
%[text] 4. Recognize the main features of an ultrasound image: speckle, dark fluid with enhancement behind it, bright interfaces, and acoustic shadows
%[text] 5. Explain why lateral resolution is worse than axial resolution
%[text] 6. Read a color Doppler image, and explain why the Doppler shift depends on the angle of the beam \
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] ## Clear workspace
clearvars
close all
%%
%[text] ## Ultrasound is sound, but ultra
%[text] Humans hear from about 20 Hz to 20 kHz, and the top of that range drops with age. Sound above 20 kHz is **ultrasound**. Medical imaging uses about 2 to 15 MHz, a hundred to a thousand times higher than we can hear.
%[text] The following plays four short tones of increasing frequency. **Turn your volume down first.** How many can you hear?
Fs = 44100; % sample rate [Hz]
t = 0 : 1/Fs : 0.8; % time vector [s]
for f = [1000 8000 14000 18000] % tone frequencies [Hz]
    disp(f + " Hz")
    sound(0.2*sin(2*pi*f*t), Fs) % 0.2 keeps the volume low
    pause(1.2)
end
%[text] - Most people can still hear 14 kHz; fewer can hear 18 kHz
%[text] - Many speakers and headphones can't play much above 18 to 20 kHz, so even if your ears could hear ultrasound, your computer couldn't play it \
%%
%[text] ## Pulse-echo: time gives depth
%[text] Ultrasound uses **echo timing** to determine how deep a structure is in the body. The ultrasound transducer sends a short pulse of sound into the tissue. When the pulse reaches a boundary between tissues, some of the sound is reflected back toward the transducer as an **echo**. The ultrasound system measures the time between sending the pulse and receiving the echo.
%[text] In soft tissue, we assume that sound travels at approximately: **c = 1540 m/s** (1.54 mm/µs).
%[text] The measured echo time includes **both parts of the trip**:
%[text] 1. **Outgoing pulse**: the pulse travels from the transducer **down to the reflector**
%[text] 2. **Returning Echo:** the echo travels **back to the transducer** \
%[text] Therefore, the depth is only half of the total distance traveled:
%[text] $ \\textrm{depth} = \\frac{c \\times t}{2} $
%[text] For example, suppose an echo returns **130 µs** after the pulse was sent.
c = 1540; % speed of sound in soft tissue [m/s]
t_echo = 130e-6; % 130e-6 echo time [s] %[control:spinner:5500]{"position":[10,13]}
%[text] The result is approximately:
depth_mm = c * t_echo / 2 * 1000 % depth of the reflector [mm]
%[text] So, an echo that returns after 130 µs came from about 10 cm deep. 
%[text] **TRY IT:** Suppose a structure is **20 cm deep**. How long would it take for the ultrasound pulse to travel to the structure **and back to the transducer**?
%[text] *Hint: Rearrange the depth equation to solve for echo time.*
%[text] $t\_{\\text{echo}} = \\frac{2 \\times \\text{depth}}{c}$
%%
%[text] ## A-mode: Echoes along a single line
%[text] In the previous section, we learned that **echo arrival time tells us how deep a structure is**. But what happens when an ultrasound pulse encounters several tissue boundaries?
%[text] Each boundary can produce its own echo. Because the boundaries are at different depths, their echoes return at different times.
%[text] **A-mode (amplitude mode)** is the simplest way to display these echoes. It plots the strength (amplitude) of each returning echo along a single ultrasound beam.
%[text] - **Peak position:** tells us when the echo returned, and therefore the depth of the boundary.
%[text] - **Peak height:** tells us how strong the returning echo is. \
%[text] **Manually Define the tissue boundaries**
%[text] Imagine an ultrasound beam traveling through tissue and encountering four boundaries at different depths.
%[text] For each boundary, we specify its depth and the relative strength of its echo. These amplitudes are simplified, illustrative values. In real tissue, echo strength depends on differences in acoustic properties between tissues, as well as attenuation and other factors. We will explore this in the next section.
interfaceDepth_cm = [1.5 2.5 5.0 5.6]; % boundary depths [cm]

interfaceName = ["fat → muscle" "muscle → liver" ...
    "liver → blood" "blood → liver"];

echoAmplitude = [0.11 0.02 0.01 0.01]; % relative echo amplitudes
simulate_a_mode(interfaceDepth_cm, interfaceName, echoAmplitude) % calls function below
%[text] **Displaying the A-mode signal**
%[text] The figure looks look at the same echoes in two ways:
%[text] - **Top plot:** Echo amplitude versus time after the pulse.
%[text] - **Bottom plot:** Echo amplitude versus depth. \
%[text] The bottom plot converts time into depth using the pulse-echo equation.
%[text] **What do you notice?**
%[text] The two plots show **exactly the same echoes**. Only the horizontal axis has changed.
%[text] - Echoes from shallow boundaries arrive first. Echoes from deeper boundaries arrive later.
%[text] - The fat–muscle boundary produces the tallest peak in our simulation.
%[text] - The height of a peak represents echo strength, **not the size or thickness of the tissue boundary**. \
%[text] **Try it**
%[text] 1. Change the depth of the first boundary from 1.5 cm to 2.5 cm. What happens to its peak in each plot?
%[text] 2. Increase the amplitude of the second echo from 0.02 to 0.08. What changes? Does its position move?
%[text] 3. If two tissue boundaries are very close together, what might happen to their echoes? \
%[text] **From A-mode to B-mode**
%[text] A-mode tells us the **depth and strength of echoes along a single line**.
%[text] But most medical ultrasound images show an entire cross-section of tissue.
%[text] **B-mode (brightness mode)** builds on the same principle. Instead of displaying echo amplitude as the height of a peak, B-mode represents echo strength as brightness. By collecting echoes along many neighboring beam lines, the ultrasound system builds a two-dimensional grayscale image.
%%
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ## Acoustic impedance: where echoes come from
%[text] **Acoustic impedance** (Z) is a tissue's density times its speed of sound. An echo forms wherever Z changes. The fraction of the sound *intensity* that reflects at a boundary between tissues 1 and 2 is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ R = \\left(\\frac{Z\_2 - Z\_1}{Z\_2 + Z\_1}\\right)^2 $
tissue = ["Air" "Lung" "Fat" "Blood" "Liver" "Muscle" "Bone"];
Z = [0.0004 0.18 1.38 1.61 1.65 1.71 7.8]; % approximate acoustic impedance [MRayl]
reflectFrom = @(Z1, Z2) ((Z2 - Z1) ./ (Z2 + Z1)).^2; % fraction of intensity reflected
R_fromMuscle = reflectFrom(Z(tissue=="Muscle"), Z); % from muscle into each tissue
table(tissue', Z', round(100*R_fromMuscle', 2), VariableNames=["Tissue" "Z (MRayl)" "% reflected from muscle"])
%[text] - Between soft tissues (fat, blood, liver, muscle), only about 1% of the sound or less reflects. Most of it continues deeper, which is what lets us image deep structures
%[text] - Muscle to bone reflects about 40%, and bone also absorbs strongly, so little sound gets past bone: it casts an **acoustic shadow**
%[text] - Muscle to air reflects almost 100%. That is why we spread **gel** between the transducer and the skin: any air in between would reflect nearly all of the sound before it entered the body \
%%
%[text] ## Acoustic impedance: Where do echoes come from?
%[text] ![](text:image:7d11)![](text:image:78f5)![](text:image:2721)
%[text] In the previous section, we simulated echoes returning from different tissue boundaries. But **why does an ultrasound pulse produce an echo when it encounters a boundary?**
%[text] The answer involves a property of tissue called **acoustic impedance**.
%[text] **What is acoustic impedance?**
%[text] Acoustic impedance (Z) describes how a material responds to a traveling sound wave. It depends on two properties:
%[text] - **Density (ρ):** how much mass is packed into a given volume.
%[text] - **Speed of sound (c):** how fast sound travels through the material. \
%[text] $Z = \\rho c$
%[text] Different tissues have different acoustic impedances. When an ultrasound pulse reaches a boundary between two tissues, some of its energy may be **reflected** back toward the transducer, while the rest continues into the next tissue. The important idea is:
%[text] **The greater the difference in acoustic impedance between two tissues, the greater the fraction of sound reflected at their boundary.**
%[text] If two tissues have identical acoustic impedances, there is no reflection from their boundary in this simplified model.
%[text] **How much sound is reflected?**
%[text] For a sound wave striking a boundary straight on, the fraction of incident sound **intensity** that is reflected is:
%[text] $ R = \\left(\\frac{Z\_2 - Z\_1}{Z\_2 + Z\_1}\\right)^2 $
%[text] where:
%[text] - (Z\_1) = acoustic impedance of the first tissue
%[text] - (Z\_2) = acoustic impedance of the second tissue
%[text] - (R) = fraction of incident sound intensity reflected \
%[text] For example, if (R=0.01), then **1% of the incident sound intensity is reflected** and approximately 99% passes into the next tissue, assuming no losses at the boundary.
%[text] **Explore reflection between tissues**
%[text] Let's compare the acoustic impedances of several tissues and calculate how much sound is reflected when an ultrasound pulse travels **from muscle into each tissue**.
tissue = ["Air" "Lung" "Fat" "Blood" "Liver" "Muscle" "Bone"];

% Approximate acoustic impedance [MRayl]
Z = [0.0004 0.18 1.38 1.61 1.65 1.71 7.8];

% Fraction of incident intensity reflected
reflectFrom = @(Z1,Z2) ((Z2-Z1)./(Z2+Z1)).^2;

% Reflection from muscle into each tissue
R_fromMuscle = reflectFrom(Z(tissue=="Muscle"), Z);

% Display reflected intensity as a percentage
reflectionTable = table(tissue', Z', ...
    round(100*R_fromMuscle',2), ...
    VariableNames=["Tissue","Z_MRayl","PercentReflected"]);

% Remove muscle (0% reflection cannot appear on a log scale)
idx = tissue ~= "Muscle";

% Create horizontal bar chart
figure
barh(categorical(tissue(idx)), 100 * R_fromMuscle(idx))

set(gca, XScale="log", FontSize=14)
xlabel('Reflected intensity (%)')
ylabel('Tissue')
title('Ultrasound Reflection at Muscle–Tissue Boundaries')
grid on

disp(reflectionTable)
%[text] **What do you notice?**
%[text] **1. Soft tissues produce relatively weak reflections.**
%[text] Fat, blood, liver, and muscle have fairly similar acoustic impedances. At their boundaries, only a small fraction of the sound intensity is reflected.
%[text] Most of the sound continues deeper into the body, allowing ultrasound to image structures beneath the surface.
%[text] **2. Bone produces strong reflections and acoustic shadows.**
%[text] At a muscle–bone boundary, approximately **41% of the incident intensity is reflected**.
%[text] Bone also strongly attenuates ultrasound, so relatively little sound reaches structures behind it.
%[text] This creates an **acoustic shadow**, a dark region in the ultrasound image where few echoes return.
%[text] **3. Air reflects almost all the sound.**
%[text] At a muscle–air boundary, nearly **100% of the incident sound intensity is reflected**.
%[text] This explains why ultrasound transducers require **coupling gel**. Even a thin layer of air between the transducer and skin would prevent most of the ultrasound energy from entering the body.
%[text] The gel eliminates air gaps and allows sound to pass efficiently from the transducer into the tissue.
%[text] **Reflection**
%[text] 1. Which tissue in the table has the acoustic impedance closest to muscle? What percentage of sound is reflected at that boundary?
%[text] 2. What happens to the reflected intensity when two tissues have exactly the same acoustic impedance? \
%[text] **Think about it:** Why might ultrasound have difficulty imaging structures located behind bone or air-filled regions?
%%
%[text] ## Attenuation and Transducer Frequency
%[text] As ultrasound travels through tissue, it gradually loses energy. This loss is called **attenuation**.
%[text] Some of the sound energy is absorbed by tissue and converted to heat, while some is scattered away from the ultrasound beam.
%[text] Two factors affect how much sound is lost:
%[text] - **Depth:** The farther sound travels, the more energy it loses.
%[text] - **Frequency:** Higher-frequency ultrasound loses energy faster than lower-frequency ultrasound. \
%[text] Remember that an ultrasound echo must travel **to a tissue boundary and back**. This means that deeper structures produce weaker returning echoes, even if they reflect the same amount of sound.**The tradeoff: Penetration versus resolution**
%[text] Why not simply use low-frequency ultrasound to image everything?
%[text] Because frequency also determines the **wavelength** of the ultrasound wave.
%[text] Higher frequencies have shorter wavelengths, which generally allow us to distinguish smaller structures. However, higher frequencies also experience greater attenuation.
%[text] This creates an important tradeoff:
%[text] - **Higher frequency:** Shorter wavelength and potentially better resolution, but less penetration.
%[text] - **Lower frequency:** Longer wavelength and potentially lower resolution, but greater penetration. \
%[text] For this reason, high-frequency transducers (approximately 10–15 MHz) are commonly used to image shallow structures such as tendons, the thyroid, and superficial blood vessels. Lower-frequency transducers (approximately 2–5 MHz) are commonly used for deeper structures, such as abdominal organs.**Explore the effect of frequency**
%[text] Let's investigate this tradeoff using two plots.
%[text] Use the slider below to change the transducer frequency from 2 to 15 MHz.
%[text] As you move the slider, observe what happens to both plots.
%[text] 1. Start at 3 MHz. How do the two transducers compare?
%[text] 2. Increase the frequency to 12 MHz. What happens to the echo strength as depth increases?
%[text] 3. Compare the wavelengths at 3 and 12 MHz. Which transducer has the shorter wavelength?
%[text] 4. Suppose you need to image a structure 15 cm deep. Would you choose a 3 MHz or a 12 MHz transducer? Explain your reasoning. \
% Change the transducer frequency using the slider
f_MHz = 4; %[control:slider:9d27]{"position":[9,10]}

% Compare penetration and wavelength
plotUltrasoundFrequency(f_MHz)
%[text] **Left plot: Penetration**
%[text] Shows how the returning echo becomes weaker as the depth of the reflecting structure increases.
%[text] **Right plot: Wavelength**
%[text] Shows how the wavelength changes with transducer frequency. Shorter wavelengths generally allow better spatial resolution.
%[text] The plots compare a fixed **3 MHz reference transducer** with a second transducer whose frequency you can change.**Try it**
%[text] **Key question:** Why can't we use a single transducer frequency to obtain both the best resolution and the greatest imaging depth?
%[text] Question 1: As you increase the transducer frequency, what happens to the echo amplitude at a depth of 10 cm?
%[text] Question 2: As you increase the frequency, what happens to the wavelength? What does this suggest about the ability to distinguish small structures?
%%
%[text] ### Decibels
%[text] Decibels are a logarithmic scale for comparing intensities: every 10 dB is a factor of 10.
dB = [3 10 20 60];
intensityRatio = 10.^(dB/10) % how many times weaker the echo is
%[text] - A 60 dB loss means the echo is a million times weaker than the pulse that was sent
%[text] - **Time gain compensation (TGC)** makes up for this: the machine amplifies echoes more the later they arrive, so deep tissue isn't drawn too dark \
%%
%[text] # Doppler Ultrasound
%[text] ## The Doppler Shift: Measuring Blood Flow
%[text] So far, we have used ultrasound echoes to determine **where structures are located** and how strongly they reflect sound.
%[text] But ultrasound can also measure **motion**, including blood flowing through vessels.
%[text] This works because of the **Doppler effect**.
%[text] **What is the Doppler effect?**
%[text] You have probably noticed that the pitch of an ambulance siren changes as it drives past you.
%[text] - As the ambulance approaches, the sound has a higher frequency (higher pitch).
%[text] - As it moves away, the sound has a lower frequency (lower pitch). \
%[text] The same principle applies to ultrasound. When an ultrasound pulse reflects from moving red blood cells, the returning echo has a slightly different frequency from the transmitted pulse.
%[text] - Blood moving **toward** the transducer produces a higher echo frequency.
%[text] - Blood moving **away** from the transducer produces a lower echo frequency. \
%[text] The difference between the transmitted and received frequencies is called the **Doppler shift**.
%[text] **Why does the angle matter?**
%[text] ![doppler angle](text:image:69c9)
%[text] 
%[text] The Doppler shift depends not only on how fast the blood is moving, but also on the **angle between the ultrasound beam and the direction of blood flow**.
%[text] $f\_D = \\frac{2f\_0v\\cos(\\theta)}{c}$
%[text] where:
%[text] - $f\_D$ = Doppler frequency shift (Hz)
%[text] - $f\_0$ = transmitted ultrasound frequency (Hz)
%[text] - $v$ = blood velocity (m/s)
%[text] - $\\theta$ = angle between the ultrasound beam and blood flow
%[text] - $c$ = speed of sound in tissue (1540 m/s) \
%[text] Notice the term ($\\cos(\\theta)$). It determines how much of the blood's motion is detected along the ultrasound beam.
%[text] **Two important cases:**
%[text] - **At 0°:** Blood moves directly along the beam. The Doppler shift is greatest.
%[text] - **At 90°:** Blood moves perpendicular to the beam. The Doppler shift is zero. \
%[text] This means that Doppler ultrasound measures **motion along the beam**, not the full velocity of blood flowing in an arbitrary direction.
%[text] **Explore the Doppler angle**
%[text] Suppose blood is flowing at 0.5 m/s (50 cm/s), and we use a 5 MHz transducer. Use the slider to change the angle between the ultrasound beam and the direction of blood flow.
f0 = 5e6;        % transducer frequency [Hz]
v = 0.5;         % blood velocity [m/s]
theta_deg = 90;   % Doppler angle [degrees] %[control:slider:773c]{"position":[13,15]}
c = 1540;        % speed of sound [m/s]

dopplerShift_Hz = 2 * f0 * v * cosd(theta_deg) / c
%[text] Start at 0° and gradually increase the angle to 90°.
%[text] **What do you notice?**
%[text] - At 0°, the Doppler shift is approximately 3,250 Hz.
%[text] - As the angle increases, the Doppler shift decreases.
%[text] - At 90°, the Doppler shift becomes zero, even though the blood is still moving. \
%%
%[text] **Listen to the Doppler shift**
%[text] Although the ultrasound transducer operates at millions of cycles per second (MHz), the Doppler frequency shift is often only a few thousand cycles per second (Hz).
%[text] These frequencies fall within the range of human hearing!
%[text] Ultrasound systems can process the Doppler signal and play it as sound, allowing clinicians to hear changes in blood flow.
%[text] Let's simulate this by playing a tone whose frequency matches the magnitude of the Doppler shift.
Fs = 12000;                  % audio sampling rate [Hz]
t = 0:1/Fs:0.5;             % 0.5-second sound
toneFrequency = abs(dopplerShift_Hz);

if toneFrequency > 1
    sound(0.2*sin(2*pi*toneFrequency*t),Fs)
end
%[text] Use the angle slider to change the Doppler shift, then rerun the audio section.
%[text] **Listen carefully:** What happens to the pitch as the angle approaches 90°?
%[text] At 90°, the Doppler shift is zero, so there is no audible tone in this simplified simulation.
%[text] *Note: Real Doppler ultrasound produces more complex sounds because blood cells move at different speeds and blood velocity changes during each heartbeat.*
%[text] **Try it**
%[text] 1. At which angle is the Doppler shift greatest?
%[text] 2. What happens to the Doppler shift when the angle changes from 0° to 60°?
%[text] 3. If blood is moving at 0.5 m/s but the Doppler angle is 90°, what velocity would the ultrasound system detect along its beam? \
%[text] **Think about it:** Why is the angle between the ultrasound beam and blood flow important when measuring blood velocity?
%%
%[text] ## The Doppler shift
%[text] When sound reflects off moving blood, the echo comes back at a slightly different frequency. Blood moving toward the transducer raises the frequency; blood moving away lowers it. The size of the shift is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\Delta f = \\frac{2 f\_0 \\, v \\cos\\theta}{c} $
%[text] where $ f\_0 $ is the transducer frequency, $ v $ is the blood velocity, and $ \\theta $ is the angle between the beam and the direction of flow. Use the slider to change the angle.
f0 = 5e6; % transducer frequency [Hz]
v = 0.5; % blood velocity [m/s]
theta_deg = 90; % angle between the beam and the flow [degrees] %[control:slider:b8e2]{"position":[13,15]}
dopplerShift_Hz = 2 * f0 * v * cosd(theta_deg) / c
%[text] - At 0°, with the beam along the flow, a 5 MHz transducer gives a shift of about 3200 Hz for blood moving at 50 cm/s
%[text] - At 90°, with the beam across the flow, the shift is zero: Doppler only measures motion along the beam
%[text] - Notice that the shift is in the range of human hearing, even though the ultrasound is not. That is why Doppler machines can play blood flow as sound \
%[text] ### Listen to the Doppler shift
%[text] Here we play the Doppler shift as a tone. (A real Doppler signal contains a spread of frequencies, because blood cells move at different speeds, and it changes with each heartbeat.)
sound(0.2*sin(2*pi*dopplerShift_Hz*t), Fs)
%[text] - Change the angle with the slider above, rerun both sections, and listen again. What happens to the pitch as the angle approaches 90°? \
%%
%[text] ### Shift vs angle
angles = 0:90;
figure;
plot(angles, 2 * f0 * v * cosd(angles) / c, LineWidth=2)
xlabel('Angle between the beam and the flow (degrees)')
ylabel('Doppler shift (Hz)')
title('The Doppler shift falls to zero at 90°')
grid on
%%
%[text] ## A color Doppler echocardiogram
%[text] So far, we have explored two important applications of ultrasound:
%[text] - **B-mode imaging:** Uses returning echoes to create a grayscale image of structures inside the body.
%[text] - **Doppler ultrasound:** Uses changes in echo frequency to detect and measure motion, such as blood flow. \
%[text] What happens when we combine these two techniques?**Seeing the heart and blood flow**
%[text] An **echocardiogram** is an ultrasound examination of the heart. It allows clinicians to observe the heart's chambers, valves, and walls as they move during each heartbeat.
%[text] A conventional B-mode echocardiogram produces a moving grayscale image of the heart's anatomy.
%[text] **Color Doppler echocardiography** adds a color overlay that shows blood flow within the heart.
%[text] The result combines two types of information in a single image:
%[text] - **Grayscale:** Shows the structure and movement of the heart.
%[text] - **Color:** Shows the direction and relative velocity of blood flow within a selected region. \
%[text] This allows clinicians to observe how blood moves through the heart and across its valves.
%%
%[text] ### **1. Load a sample echocardiogram**
%[text] MATLAB includes a sample color Doppler echocardiogram stored as a DICOM file.
%[text] This example contains a short sequence of ultrasound images, called a **cine loop**. Each frame shows the heart at a slightly different moment in time.
close all
clearvars
info = dicominfo("US-PAL-8-10x-echo.dcm");
[echo, echoMap] = dicomread(info);
size(echo)
info.NumberOfFrames
info.FrameTime % time between frames [ms]
framesPerSecond = 1000 / info.FrameTime
%[text] **What do we learn from the metadata?**
%[text] - The cine loop contains **10 frames**.
%[text] - Each frame is **430 × 600 pixels**.
%[text] - The images are indexed, meaning that pixel values refer to colors stored in the colormap, `echoMap`.
%[text] - The frame timing corresponds to approximately **13 frames per second**. \
%[text] Unlike an ordinary RGB image, an indexed image stores pixel values that refer to entries in a separate colormap. Here, the colormap contains the grayscale values and colors used to display the ultrasound image.
%%
%[text] **2. Display a single frame**
%[text] Let's examine the first frame of the echocardiogram.
figure;
imshow(echo(:,:,:,1), echoMap)
title('Color Doppler echocardiogram, frame 1')
%[text] **What are we looking at?**
%[text] The image combines anatomical information with blood flow measurements.
%[text] ![](text:image:46b3)
%[text] **The grayscale image**
%[text] The grayscale portion is a B-mode ultrasound image showing a cross-sectional view of the heart. Brighter regions generally correspond to stronger returning echoes, while darker regions correspond to weaker echoes. Look for the outlines of the heart walls and the darker spaces inside the heart chambers, which contain blood.
%[text] **The color Doppler overlay**
%[text] Within a selected region of the image, colors represent blood flow detected by Doppler ultrasound.
%[text] The colors indicate the direction of blood movement **relative to the ultrasound transducer**.
%[text] A common color convention is:
%[text] - **Red:** Blood moving toward the transducer.
%[text] - **Blue:** Blood moving away from the transducer. \
%[text] An easy way to remember this is **BART: Blue Away, Red Toward**.
%[text] The color scale displayed on the image indicates how the colors correspond to Doppler velocities. Different shades represent different measured velocities, according to that scale.
%[text] **Important:** Red and blue do not identify arteries and veins. They indicate the direction of blood flow relative to the transducer.
%[text] Also remember that Doppler measures only the component of blood velocity along the ultrasound beam.
%%
%[text] ### **3. Watch the heart in motion**
%[text] A single ultrasound frame provides a snapshot of the heart. However, the heart is constantly moving as it contracts and relaxes.
%[text] To observe this motion, we can display the frames in sequence as a short movie, or **cine loop**.
figure
h = imshow(echo(:,:,:,1), echoMap);
title('Color Doppler Echocardiogram')

for k = 1:info.NumberOfFrames
    h.CData = echo(:,:,:,k);
    title("Frame " + k)
    pause(info.FrameTime/1000)
end
%[text] As the movie plays, observe how the grayscale structures and color patterns change from frame to frame.
%[text] The grayscale image shows the motion of the heart, while the color overlay reveals changes in blood flow during the cardiac cycle.
%[text] **Try it**
%[text] 1. **Anatomy:** Identify a dark region that might represent a blood-filled heart chamber. What structures surround it?
%[text] 2. **Blood flow:** Find regions displayed in red and blue. What do these colors tell you about the direction of blood flow?
%[text] 3. **Motion:** As you watch the cine loop, how do the grayscale structures and color patterns change?
%[text] 4. **Interpretation:** If a region changes from red to blue, what might that indicate about the direction of blood flow relative to the transducer? \
%[text] **Think about it:** Why is combining B-mode and color Doppler more informative than using either technique alone?
%%
figure
imshow(echo(:,:,:,1), echoMap)
title('Color Doppler Echocardiogram')
%%
%[text] # Reading Ultrasound Images
%[text] ## The dataset: 3D ultrasound of a phantom and a forearm
%[text] These volumes come from a Modern Human Anatomy capstone project by Jonathan Dolata (2016), who tested whether 3D ultrasound could be used to build 3D models of the forearm muscles. They were acquired with Dr. Ann Scherzinger (Radiology) on a Philips iU22 scanner with a 3D linear probe (VL13-5, 5 to 13 MHz). The probe rocks its image plane back and forth as it scans, so each file holds a stack of 256 frames: a 3D volume.
%[text] - Two volumes are of a **phantom**: a block of gel built to look like soft tissue on ultrasound, with targets of known shape inside. Phantoms are used to test and calibrate ultrasound machines
%[text] - One volume is of a **forearm**: the front (anterior) of the student's own forearm, with the forearm supinated (palm up) and the probe on the skin with gel \
mmSetUnitDataFolder(3); % change Current Folder to the Unit 3 Data Folder
dataFolder = "US3D";
cysts = squeeze(dicomread(fullfile(dataFolder, "PhantomCysts.dcm")));
wires = squeeze(dicomread(fullfile(dataFolder, "PhantomWires.dcm")));
forearm = squeeze(dicomread(fullfile(dataFolder, "Forearm.dcm")));
size(cysts)
%[text] - Each volume is depth × width × frame: 367 rows going down from the probe, 192 columns across the probe face, and 256 frames in the sweep
%[text] - These files don't record the pixel size, so we will work in pixels \
%%
%[text] ### Display one frame
%[text] Display frame from Phantom
frameNumber = 128; % a frame from the middle of the sweep
cystFrame = cysts(:,:,frameNumber);
figure;
imshow(cystFrame)
title("Phantom with cysts, frame " + frameNumber)
impixelinfo
%[text] Look for these features of an ultrasound image:
%[text] - **The probe is at the top.** Row 1 is at the skin (here, the surface of the gel), and each row down is deeper
%[text] - **Speckle.** The grainy texture comes from sound scattering off structures smaller than the wavelength. It is not noise in the usual sense, and it gives each tissue its texture
%[text] - **Dark (anechoic) circles.** These are fluid-filled cylinders in the gel, which stand in for cysts. Fluid has few scatterers, so it sends back almost no echo
%[text] - The image gets darker toward the bottom. We will come back to this in the next section \
%%
%[text] ### Are the pixels square?
%[text] The cysts are round cylinders, but in the image they look taller than they are wide. Here we measure the largest one.
cystMask = imgaussfilt(double(cystFrame), 3) < 40; % dark pixels (smoothed, to ignore speckle)
cystMask([1:250 335:end], :) = false; % keep only the rows with the cysts
cystProps = regionprops("table", cystMask, "BoundingBox", "Area");
cystProps = sortrows(cystProps, "Area", "descend");
cystBox = cystProps.BoundingBox(1,:) % [left top width height] of the largest cyst [pixels]
pixelAspect = cystBox(4) / cystBox(3) % pixel width / pixel height
%[text] - The largest cyst is 46 pixels tall but only 29 pixels wide. Since it is really round, each pixel must be about 1.6 times as wide as it is tall
%[text] - A shape you already know is a simple way to calibrate an image. Here we use pixelAspect to draw the image with the right proportions \
figure;
tiledlayout("horizontal")
nexttile
imshow(cystFrame)
daspect([1 pixelAspect 1]) % stretch the image sideways so the pixels have the right shape
title("Phantom with cysts, correct proportions")
%%
%[text] ## Attenuation you can see
%[text] The deeper the sound travels, the more it is absorbed and scattered, so the echoes from deep tissue are weaker. Here we average the brightness of each row (each depth), over a strip of the phantom with no cysts in it and over all 256 frames.
strip = double(cysts(:, 10:45, :)); % columns 10 to 45 contain no cysts
rowMean = mean(strip, [2 3]); % average brightness at each depth
nexttile;
plot(rowMean, LineWidth=2)
xlabel('Depth (pixel row)')
ylabel('Average brightness')
title('Brightness vs depth in the phantom')
grid on
%[text] - The bright band at the very top is the probe face and the gel against it
%[text] - Below that, the brightness rises to a peak about 100 rows down, near where the beam is focused
%[text] - Deeper than that, the brightness falls steadily, to about one eighth of the peak at the bottom. The gel is the same all the way down, so this fall comes from **attenuation**
%[text] - The machine was already applying time gain compensation (TGC), and it still wasn't enough to keep the deep gel as bright as the shallow gel \
%%
%[text] ### Apply your own Gain
%[text] Time gain compensation, TGC,  amplifies the echoes more the deeper they come from. Here we make a TGC curve from the measured fall-off: below the peak, each row is multiplied by however much it needs to match the peak brightness.
[peakBrightness, peakRow] = max(rowMean(20:end)); % skip the bright band at the top
peakRow = peakRow + 19;
tgc = ones(size(rowMean));
tgc(peakRow:end) = peakBrightness ./ rowMean(peakRow:end); % gain needed at each depth
tgc = min(tgc, 10); % cap the gain (the last few rows are almost black)
corrected = double(cystFrame) .* tgc; % multiply each row by its gain
figure;
tiledlayout(1,3, TileSpacing="compact")
nexttile
imshow(cystFrame)
daspect([1 pixelAspect 1])
title('As acquired')
nexttile
plot(tgc, 1:numel(tgc), LineWidth=2)
set(gca, 'YDir', 'reverse')
xlabel('Gain')
ylabel('Depth (pixel row)')
title('TGC curve')
grid on
nexttile
imshow(uint8(corrected))
daspect([1 pixelAspect 1])
title('With extra TGC')
%[text] - The corrected image is about as bright at the bottom as at the top
%[text] - Look closely at the bottom: TGC amplifies everything at that depth, including the electronic noise, so the deepest part of the image gets noisier, not more detailed. TGC can't bring back signal that never returned \
%%
%[text] ## Cysts: dark inside, bright behind
%[text] Here we zoom in on the cysts. To make it easier to see past the speckle, we average 21 frames from the middle of the sweep (the cysts are long cylinders, so they are in the same place in every frame).
cystAvg = mean(double(cysts(:,:,118:138)), 3);
figure;
imshow(cystAvg(230:end, :), [0 200])
daspect([1 pixelAspect 1])
title('The three cysts (average of 21 frames)')
%[text] - The cysts are black inside: fluid sends back almost no echo
%[text] - Look at the gel **directly below** the largest cyst. It is brighter than the gel beside it at the same depth. This is **posterior acoustic enhancement**: the sound that passed through the fluid lost less energy than the sound that passed through the gel beside it, so the echoes from behind the cyst are stronger. You will measure it in Exercise 4
%[text] - Clinically, a dark structure with enhancement behind it suggests a fluid-filled cyst rather than a solid mass
%[text] - Can you  see the smallest cyst? Small, low-contrast targets are the first to disappear in speckle \
%%
%[text] ## Point targets: how sharp is the image?
%[text] The second phantom contains thin nylon wires that run straight through the gel, perpendicular to the image. Each wire should look like a tiny point. How big the points look tells us the **resolution** of the system.
wireFrame = wires(:,:,frameNumber);
figure;
imshow(wireFrame)
daspect([1 pixelAspect 1])
title("Phantom with wires, frame " + frameNumber)
%[text] - Near the top, the wires look like small dots
%[text] - Near the bottom, there are two groups of wires that are close together. Let's zoom in \
wireGroup = wireFrame(270:330, 85:150);
figure;
imshow(wireGroup, InitialMagnification=600)
daspect([1 pixelAspect 1])
title('Two groups of closely spaced wires')
%[text] - In the lower group, the wires run diagonally: each is a little deeper than the last, and you can count each one
%[text] - In the upper right, several wires sit **side by side** at the same depth, and they blur into one bright dash \
%[text] Here we plot the brightness along the row through the upper group.
wireRow = 283;
figure;
plot(85:150, wireFrame(wireRow, 85:150), LineWidth=2)
xlabel('Column (pixel)')
ylabel('Brightness')
title("Brightness along row " + wireRow)
grid on
%[text] - The isolated wire on the left (around column 117) gives a single, clear peak
%[text] - The group on the right gives one wide plateau with only small dips. The wires are barely resolved: you can just make out separate bumps \
%[text] Why do wires side by side blur together, but wires one above the other don't?
%[text] - **Axial resolution** (along the beam, in depth) depends on the length of the pulse. A short pulse, from a high frequency probe, separates two reflectors one behind the other
%[text] - **Lateral resolution** (across the image) depends on the width of the beam. The beam is wider than the pulse is long, and it widens away from the focus, so lateral resolution is worse than axial resolution, and gets worse with depth
%[text] - There is a third direction: the thickness of the image plane (elevation). It is usually the worst of the three. You will see it when you explore the volume in 3D \
%%
%[text] ## US of a Forearm: Bone and the acoustic shadow
%[text] Each frame of the forearm volume is a **longitudinal** view: the probe was lined up along the long axis of the arm, so the image plane runs along the arm. If we take one column from every frame, we get a **cross-section** of the arm instead. This view is fan-shaped, because the probe tilts its image plane as it sweeps.
longView = forearm(:,:,frameNumber); % one frame: along the arm
crossView = squeeze(forearm(:,96,:)); % one column from every frame: across the arm
figure;
tiledlayout(1,2, TileSpacing="compact")
nexttile
imshow(longView)
daspect([1 pixelAspect 1])
title('Forearm, along the arm')
nexttile
imshow(crossView)
title('Forearm, across the arm')
%[text] - **Along the arm**, the muscles show long, bright lines: the fascia (connective tissue) between muscles and between bundles of muscle fibers. The scanned region of the anterior forearm includes muscles such as brachioradialis, the wrist flexors and extensors, flexor digitorum superficialis, pronator teres, and supinator
%[text] - **Across the arm**, the muscles are cut in cross-section, and the bright curved lines on the left and near the bottom are **bone surfaces**
%[text] - Below each bone surface, the image is dark: an **acoustic shadow** \
%[text] Here we plot the brightness along two beams in the cross-section: one that hits the bone, and one that passes beside it.
boneBeams = 55:65; % columns that cross the bone
otherBeams = 155:165; % columns away from the bone at that depth
boneProfile = mean(double(crossView(:, boneBeams)), 2); % average a few neighboring beams to smooth the speckle
otherProfile = mean(double(crossView(:, otherBeams)), 2);
figure;
plot(boneProfile, LineWidth=1.5)
hold on
plot(otherProfile, LineWidth=1.5)
hold off
xlabel('Depth (pixel row)')
ylabel('Brightness')
legend("Beams that hit the bone", "Beams beside the bone")
title('The acoustic shadow behind bone')
grid on
%[text] - The beams that hit the bone have a bright peak at the bone surface (around row 200). Below it, they are much darker than the beams beside the bone at the same depth
%[text] - The beams beside the bone keep going until they reach a second bone surface, much deeper (around row 335), with a shadow below it too
%[text] - About 41% of the sound reflects at a muscle–bone boundary (you will calculate this in Exercise 2), and bone absorbs much of the rest. Little sound gets past, so there are almost no echoes from behind the bone
%[text] - Shadows are useful clinically: they help find bone, gallstones, and kidney stones. They also hide anything behind them
%[text] - In the capstone project, the muscles right next to bone were the hardest to outline: the strong echoes and shadows from bone hid their borders \
%%
%[text] ## Explore the volumes in 3D
%[text] Scroll through the forearm in all three directions. The three panels show the three directions through the volume: depth × width (one frame), depth × sweep, and width × sweep.
orthosliceViewer(forearm, DisplayRange=[0 255]);
%[text] - Which direction looks the blurriest? (Hint: think about elevation) \
%[text] Here we render the forearm in 3D, stretched sideways by pixelAspect so the frames have the right proportions.
viewer = viewer3d;
volshow(forearm, Parent=viewer, Transformation=affinetform3d(diag([pixelAspect 1 1 1])));
%[text] - Try the other volumes (cysts and wires). In the cyst phantom, can you find the cylinders?
%[text] - Why is ultrasound harder to render in 3D than CT? (Hint: think about speckle, and about how much brighter bone is than soft tissue on CT) \
%[text] ### What the capstone project found
%[text] To build the 3D muscle models, the student had to outline each muscle by hand, slice by slice, in segmentation software. Several of the reasons connect to ideas in this script:
%[text] - **Automatic segmentation failed.** Speckle and artifacts gave the muscles no consistent brightness, and a border that was clear in one slice could vanish in the next. Every muscle was traced by hand, using the other viewing planes to guess where the borders were
%[text] - **Deep muscles were harder than superficial ones.** Their borders were less distinct, because the echoes from deep tissue are weaker and the beam is wider (see the Attenuation and Point targets sections)
%[text] - **Muscles next to bone were hard to outline**, because of the bright echoes and shadows from bone
%[text] - **Tendons were nearly impossible.** Tendons are **anisotropic**: they are bright only when the beam hits them straight on, and turn dark when it hits them at an angle. In a live scan, the sonographer can tilt the probe to check; a stored volume has only one angle
%[text] - **Pressing harder changed the anatomy.** Different pressure on the probe squashed the superficial muscles by different amounts in each scan, so neighboring scans did not line up when the student tried to combine them into one model
%[text] - Which of these problems would you not have with a CT or MRI of the same forearm? \
%%
